10.2 Parameterized queries and SQL injection
This section shows why a program must never build SQL out of the values it is given, and what it does instead.
A program that searches for a lot receives the lot number from somewhere: a text box, a file, a scanned label. The obvious way to use it is to paste it into the SQL text, and Kotlin’s string templates make that easy:

/** Wrong: the lot number becomes part of the SQL text. */
fun countMeasurementsUnsafely(
    connection: Connection, lotNumber: String
): Int {
    val sql = "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM quality.measurement AS m " +
        "JOIN quality.inspection AS i " +
        "ON i.inspection_pk = m.inspection_fk " +
        "WHERE i.lot_number = '$lotNumber'"
    connection.createStatement().use { statement -&gt;
        statement.executeQuery(sql).use { rows -&gt;
            rows.next()
            return rows.getInt(1)
        }
    }
}

The function builds the query ... WHERE i.lot_number = 'L7005-03' and runs it, and for an ordinary lot number it works. It works, that is, until the lot number contains a quote. The value then ends the SQL string early, and what follows it is read as SQL. The program LotSearch runs the function on three inputs:

val inputs = listOf("L7005-03", "L7005-03' OR '1'='1", "L7005'03")

It then runs a second function on the same inputs, which differs from the first only in its last line of SQL, WHERE i.lot_number = ?, and in supplying the lot number with setString:

/** Right: the lot number is a parameter, sent apart from the SQL. */
fun countMeasurements(
    connection: Connection, lotNumber: String
): Int {
    val sql = "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM quality.measurement AS m " +
        "JOIN quality.inspection AS i " +
        "ON i.inspection_pk = m.inspection_fk " +
        "WHERE i.lot_number = ?"
    connection.prepareStatement(sql).use { statement -&gt;
        statement.setString(1, lotNumber)
        statement.executeQuery().use { rows -&gt;
            rows.next()
            return rows.getInt(1)
        }
    }
}

The two functions print:

Pasted into the SQL
  L7005-03 -&gt; 2 measurements
  L7005-03' OR '1'='1 -&gt; 34 measurements
  L7005'03 -&gt; Unterminated string literal started at position 142 in SQL SELECT COUNT(*) FROM quality.measurement AS m JOIN quality.inspection AS i ON i.inspection_pk = m.inspection_fk WHERE i.lot_number = 'L7005'03'. Expected  char
As a parameter
  L7005-03 -&gt; 2 measurements
  L7005-03' OR '1'='1 -&gt; 0 measurements
  L7005'03 -&gt; 0 measurements

The first input is a real lot, and both functions count its two measurements. The second is an attack. Pasted into the SQL, it makes the condition i.lot_number = 'L7005-03' OR '1'='1', which is true of every row, and the query counts all 34 measurements of stage Q: the input has changed what the query does. The third input is merely a lot number with a mistyped apostrophe, and pasted into the SQL it leaves a string unterminated; the message, from the PostgreSQL driver, shows the broken SQL the function built. The second function treats each input as what it is, a lot number, finds no such lot, and counts zero.
Changing a query’s meaning through the values supplied to it is called SQL injection, and it is among the most exploited weaknesses of real software. It is not limited to reading: an input that ends one statement and begins another can delete or change rows, with the program’s privileges. The prepared statement prevents it by construction. The SQL, with its ?, reaches the database first and is parsed; the values follow separately, and the database uses each as a value, never as SQL, whatever characters it contains. The same mechanism handles the ordinary case correctly: a customer named Crowley's Ridge Hardware needs no doubled quote (Section 8.4) when it is a parameter.
The rule is simple and has no exceptions: a value that comes from outside the program goes into SQL only as a parameter. Build the SQL text from constants alone, and supply every value with setString, setInt, setBigDecimal, or setObject. A table or column name cannot be a parameter; if a program must choose one at run time, it chooses from a fixed list written in the program, never from its input. The next section organizes a program’s statements.

E.2 Diagrams as text: PlantUML and Graphviz
Every diagram in this book is written as text. A diagram is a small file that names the classes, their attributes, and the associations between them, and a program lays it out and draws it. Writing a diagram as text has four advantages that matter for engineering work. The file sits beside the SQL and code it describes, in the same project, under the same version control, so a change to the model is recorded with the change to the tables. A diagram can be generated by a program, as this book’s product structure trees and schema diagram are generated from its database. One shared style file makes every diagram look alike. And nothing needs dragging into place: the layout program places the boxes.
PlantUML (plantuml.com) is the tool for UML and crow’s foot diagrams. It is free, written in Java, and draws class, object, sequence, and many other UML diagrams, and entity diagrams in crow’s foot notation. The class model of stage D4, Figure E.1, is drawn from this file:
@startuml
!include _style.iuml
' Stage D4: a second association class.
class Customer {
  customerNumber
  name
  city
  state
}
class CustomerOrder {
  orderNumber
  orderDate
  requestedShipDate [0..1]
}
class OrderLine {
  lineNumber
  quantity
  unitPrice
}
class Product {
  productNumber
  description
  listPrice
}
class Warehouse {
  warehouseCode
  city
  state
}
class StockLevel {
  quantityOnHand
  reorderPoint
}
Customer "1  " -- "0..*  " CustomerOrder
CustomerOrder "1  " *-- "1..*  " OrderLine
OrderLine "0..*  " -- "1  " Product
Warehouse "0..*  " -- "0..*  " Product
(Warehouse, Product) .. StockLevel
@enduml






Figure E.1: Stage D4’s Class Model, Drawn from the File Above



Read the file against Figure E.1. Each class block is a class with its attributes. "1  " -- "0..*  " is an association with its multiplicities, written at the ends they belong to (Section 5.4); the spaces inside the quotes are optional padding that moves a label clear of its line. *-- is a composition, its filled diamond at the first class. The last two lines draw the association class: an association between Warehouse and Product, and StockLevel attached to it with a dashed line. A qualifier is written in square brackets, CustomerOrder [lineNumber] "\n1  " *-- "0..1" OrderLine, with the composition’s 1 at the whole end; the \n and the trailing spaces push that 1 below and beside the qualifier’s box, where it does not cover the diamond. A generalization is written Part &lt;|-- ManufacturedPart. These are the constructs that simpler tools cannot draw, and the reason this book chose PlantUML.
The same tool draws crow’s foot diagrams. The stock levels of Figure D.2 come from this file, whose ||--o{ is a line with two bars at one end and a circle with a crow’s foot at the other (Table D.1):
@startuml
!include _style.iuml
hide stereotype
' Stage D4's stock levels in crow's foot notation: the association class
' StockLevel as an associative entity (translation appendix).
entity WAREHOUSE {
  * warehouse_code
  --
  city
  state
}
entity STOCK_LEVEL {
  * warehouse_code (FK)
  * product_number (FK)
  --
  quantity_on_hand
  reorder_point
}
entity PRODUCT {
  * product_number
  --
  description
  list_price
}
WAREHOUSE ||--o{ STOCK_LEVEL
STOCK_LEVEL }o--|| PRODUCT
@enduml
The first line of each file after @startuml includes the book’s style file, which is why every figure in the book looks alike:
' Shared style for every diagram in the book. Included by each .puml with
'   !include _style.iuml
' Changing this file marks every diagram stale; rerun scripts/build-diagrams.sh.
!theme plain
hide circle
hide empty methods
skinparam classAttributeIconSize 0
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
' Diagram text at about the size of the book's body text.
scale 1.2
PlantUML lays out a class diagram with Graphviz (graphviz.org), a separate, free program for drawing graphs, which you install beside it. Without Graphviz, PlantUML falls back to a layout engine of its own, whose layouts differ and, in this book’s experience, mishandle qualifiers; the book’s build therefore requires Graphviz. Graphviz is also useful on its own, for diagrams that are not UML: the product structure trees of Section 4.6, the loop of a recursive query in Figure 4.8, and the dependency diagrams of Chapter 7 are Graphviz graphs. Its language, DOT, names nodes and the edges between them:
@startdot
digraph loop {
  // How a recursive query runs: the anchor once, then the recursive part
  // again and again on the rows of the previous step, until a step finds no
  // rows. Drawn by hand. Graphviz diagrams set their own fonts, matching the
  // Helvetica of _style.iuml, which applies to UML diagrams only.
  graph [rankdir=TB, nodesep=0.4, ranksep=0.35, fontname="Helvetica"];
  node  [shape=box, style="rounded,filled", fillcolor="#f2f2f2",
         fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=11, penwidth=1];
  edge  [fontname="Helvetica", fontsize=10, arrowsize=0.7];

  anchor  [label="Anchor\nrun once: find the starting rows\n(the HT-100)"];
  add     [label="Add the new rows to the result,\nand make them the working table", fillcolor="#dde8f3"];
  step    [label="Recursive part\njoin the working table to bom_line\nto find the next rows", fillcolor="#e3f0dc"];
  bom     [label="bom_line", shape=cylinder, style="filled", fillcolor="white"];
  any     [label="Any new\nrows?", shape=diamond, style="filled", fillcolor="white"];
  stop    [label="Stop: the result holds\nthe rows of every step", fillcolor="#f6e6d4"];

  anchor -&gt; add;
  add -&gt; step;
  bom -&gt; step [style=dashed, arrowhead=none];
  step -&gt; any;
  any -&gt; add [label=" yes"];
  any -&gt; stop [label=" no"];

  { rank=same; step; bom }
}
@enddot
Three ways of running PlantUML suit a student. The PlantUML plugins for IntelliJ IDEA and for VS Code draw a preview beside the file as you type, which is the easiest way to learn the notation. The command line renders files to images, as this book’s build does (java -jar plantuml.jar -tsvg file.puml). And Quarto documents, like this book, can include the rendered images in a report. PlantUML’s web site also offers an online server that renders a diagram pasted into a browser. It is convenient for a homework problem, but the text you paste leaves your computer, so you should not paste an employer’s schema or data model into it.
Mermaid (mermaid.js.org) is a lighter text notation with class diagrams and entity-relationship diagrams, and it needs no Java: GitHub renders it in Markdown files, and Quarto renders it in documents. The stock levels as a Mermaid entity-relationship diagram are:
erDiagram
    WAREHOUSE ||--o{ STOCK_LEVEL : holds
    PRODUCT ||--o{ STOCK_LEVEL : "is stocked as"
    STOCK_LEVEL {
        int quantity_on_hand
        int reorder_point
    }
Mermaid suits a quick diagram in a README or a report. Its class diagrams, however, have no association classes and no qualifiers, so the models of Chapter 5 cannot all be drawn in it; that is why this book uses PlantUML.
